12:07:48 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :you're implying that into the spec, it doesn't say that at all
12:07:59 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :ACTION bashes his head into the brick wall that is ais523.
12:08:00 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :the spec says what k does, not what k at (5,10) does
12:08:20 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :ais523: some instructions in Funge-98 make no fucking sense and require pieces of state that no other part of the language does
12:08:25 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :this should not surprise you.
12:08:30 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :it's defining the concept of k instructions (think class in OO programming), not a specific k instruction individually (think object in OO programming)
12:08:31 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :and it's not a reason to discount an interpretation
12:08:42 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :I'm just reading the plain language of what it says
12:08:42 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :the fact that doing it that way is insane is irrelevant
12:08:46 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :the question is what the spec literally says
12:08:50 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :which is ambiguous in this instance
12:08:57 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :hmm, what are instructions defined /as/?
12:09:03 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :they're not
12:09:09 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :at all?
12:09:23 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :well
12:09:24 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :All standard instructions are one character long and range from ASCII 32 (space) to ASCII 126 (~). There are no multicharacter instructions in Funge.
12:09:29 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :ais523: it tells you a lot of things about instructions
12:09:31 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :but not what an instruction actually is
12:09:34 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :An instruction is executed by an IP every tick. The IP executed is the one at the current position of the IP. Only after that does the IP moves by its delta to a new position.
12:09:35 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :etc.
12:09:58 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :hmm, why is the funge-98 spec not linked from the wiki?
12:10:10 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :elliott: they range from 32 to 126
12:10:16 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :do any of those numbers contain position metadata?
12:10:23 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :*sigh*
12:10:42 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :Where the k is is part of execution state, not instruction state. The specification creates plenty of tracking of additional state without explicitly stating that it is doing so.
12:10:51 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :elliott: you can't claim that "instruction" includes "position in the source"
12:10:54 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :It does so with off-hand notes, e.g. fields of y that require whole-interpreter tracking to satisfy.
12:11:13 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :The spec is incredibly ambiguous and you're basically saying "don't be silly, that would require things to be insane to make sense", but they _are_.
12:11:14 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :ais523: http://catseye.tc/projects/funge98/doc/funge98.html
12:11:35 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :what I'm saying is, that "next instruction" can't have an implied "after the original position in the fungespace that that copy of the instruction was copied from", because "instruction" is specified not to include that data
