12:22:22 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :Deewiant: I don't want an execution trace, I want a justification of it, because as far as I can tell implementing that in my k would be non-trivial because it makes no sense at all
12:22:38 :Deewiant!~deewiant@cs186230.pp.htv.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :The IP moves forward to get to the next cell.
12:22:41 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :because all instructions move the IP forwards when executed?
12:22:55 :Deewiant!~deewiant@cs186230.pp.htv.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :If the argument is nonzero, it finds the next instruction and executes it N times.
12:22:59 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :oh, you're treating "finds the next instruction in the path of the IP" as actually moving the IP?
12:23:01 :Deewiant!~deewiant@cs186230.pp.htv.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :If the argument is zero, it instead does nothing.
12:23:09 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :now /that's/ insane
12:23:11 :Deewiant!~deewiant@cs186230.pp.htv.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :Because 0kx is specced as skipping the x.
12:23:14 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :I didn't even realise that was a valid interpretation
12:23:23 :Deewiant!~deewiant@cs186230.pp.htv.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :It doesn't matter whether it actually moves the IP or not.
12:23:32 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :Deewiant: oh, you're right
12:23:33 :Deewiant!~deewiant@cs186230.pp.htv.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :The IP moves back before anything can tell the difference.
12:23:44 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :that bit of the spec is completely contradictory to anything else in it, and I remember you complaining about it at the time
12:23:57 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :so were you just trying to find an interpretation that complied with everything the spec said, somehow?
12:24:08 :Deewiant!~deewiant@cs186230.pp.htv.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :But the thing with nested k is that when the stack runs out and you start hitting zeroes, every execution of the inner k will move the IP forward like a #.
12:24:26 :Deewiant!~deewiant@cs186230.pp.htv.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :Pretty much, yes, that's been the idea. :-P
12:24:31 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :because k, for some reason, acts like # if it pops a 0
12:24:40 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :and you're treating that as a special case because the spec says so?
12:24:50 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :in that case, I don't think you actually disagree with elliott in anything
12:24:59 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :Deewiant: I think I've figured out why Vorpal wanted to make a new specification.
