14:47:46 :Ilari!~user@2002:5870:38d7::1 PRIVMSG #esoteric :Do either of those support IPv6? :-)
14:47:48 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :ais523: It's not serious picking!
14:47:59 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :It's just gosh-fizzie-truly-is-a-pathetic-excuse-for-a-hu maybe I won't finish this sentence!!
14:48:01 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :MAYBE I WON'T
14:48:03 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :Ilari: CLC-INTERCAL supports IPv6 nowadays
14:48:03 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :Ilari: No; Vorpal's NSCK should, though.
14:48:19 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :and the way it does it is beautifully insane too, as it's transparent for IPv4 applications
14:48:29 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :ais523: heh, how does it do it? maybe i'll implement it in SOCK and SCKE :D
14:48:36 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :SCKE just adds a v4-only gethostbyname() instruction ('H') and a poll command ('P').
14:48:36 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :the way it works is, if you DNS lookup an IPv6 address, it returns an IPv4 address in the multicast region
14:48:50 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :and then translates between them NAT-style
14:48:59 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :umm, until the application exits, I think
14:49:17 :poiuy_qwert!~poiuy_qwe@CPE001b115db0ae-CM0018c0c24ffc.cpe.net.cable.rogers.com QUIT :Client Quit
14:49:23 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :so IPv4 programs can use IPv6 without any modification at all
14:49:54 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :fizzie: hmm, what does H's return look like?
14:50:28 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :elliott: Whatever I returns; usually an integer-mapped IPv4 address.
14:50:31 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :I suggest the CLC-INTERCAL method be adopted in all other esolangs too, also real-life operating systems
14:50:40 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :ais523: There's a Internet-Draft for that mechanism.
14:50:47 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :ais523: http://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-liu-behave-nat46-02
14:50:51 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :fizzie: what if the hostname ends with %ipv6, it returns instead an IPv6 address?
14:51:01 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :CLC-INTERCAL: Implementing innovative Internet-Drafts.
14:51:37 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :hmm, that one isn't numbered
14:51:42 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :hasn't even been requested for comment yet
14:51:48 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :I wonder if clc came up with the idea independently?
14:52:17 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :elliott: If you have bignum cells, maybe. The problem with retrofitting IPv6 into SOCK is that the addresses won't fit in single cells; so you either use some sort of a handle system (with the garbage-collection problems) or have a problem.
14:52:23 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :also, those page breaks are irritating
14:52:31 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :fizzie: I'd just push two cells.
14:52:34 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :and the draft is expired (on sep 8 2010)
14:52:35 :elliott!~elliott@unaffiliated/elliott PRIVMSG #esoteric :Because, you know, radical.
14:52:42 :ais523!~ais523@unaffiliated/ais523 PRIVMSG #esoteric :fizzie: how did you find that so quickly? or even know it existed?
14:52:58 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric :ais523: Purely based on the far more popular (and sane) NAT64/DNS64 approach.
