18:30:39 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric-minecraft :Yes.
18:30:42 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric-minecraft :"A JNI call is very slow, in the order of .5 to 1.0 microseconds, the equivalent of pages of linear Java code to do a simple method call. You would think there would be a tiny generated machine code thunk to bridge between Java and C. Not so — at least in any JVM I know of, Java branches to some general purpose code that interpretively constructs the C parameters. This code is not highly optimised. It seems Sun wants to strongly discourage you from
18:30:42 :fizzie!fis@iris.zem.fi PRIVMSG #esoteric-minecraft :using native methods just for speed. This means you don’t want to hop back and forth between Java and C, but rather to go to C, and stay there a decently long time before returning. This means that you can’t use C to speed up short operations, only long ones, because of the overhead tacked on in getting to C wipes out any savings."
